diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..7b2796187df700e0126deaf96b480ef933d9c360
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,174 @@
+# arXiv:0806.3680 problem generator
+
+## Profile and trust status
+
+| field | value |
+|---|---|
+| track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| native domain | algebra |
+| object regime | integer lattice |
+| computational core | other (exact monomial divisibility and support structure) |
+| certificate | exact symbolic sparse monomial |
+| intuition | invariant: affine residues of the pure-power caps reveal the concealed coordinate classes |
+| domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+| candidate shipping preset | `hard` |
+
+The generator and every local gate pass. The required external hardness claim is
+**not yet established**: on 2026-09-05 the repository-owned hardening harness
+made four bare, four hinted, and four placebo calls, but the supplied OpenRouter
+credential returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” every time. Errors correctly
+count as neither solved nor failed. The harness-owned transcripts are retained,
+but this result must not be submitted until a funded key produces a real
+`hardened` verdict.
+
+## The problem
+
+The source is Bjarke Hammersholt Roune, [“The Slice Algorithm For Irreducible
+Decomposition of Monomial Ideals”](https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3680). The solver
+receives the complete minimal generating set of an artinian monomial ideal:
+pure powers `x_i^(c_i+1)` and explicitly listed quadratic monomials `x_i*x_j`.
+It must return one maximal standard monomial `d`, meaning `d` is outside the
+ideal while every `d*x_i` is inside it. Section 2.2 identifies these monomials
+as a socle basis and maps them bijectively to irreducible components. The answer
+is a sparse list of its eight nonzero variable/exponent pairs.
+
+Verification is independent of the plant. It checks the answer grammar and
+caps, proves no displayed generator divides `d`, and proves a displayed
+generator divides each `d*x_i`. These are exact integer comparisons; no CAS,
+floating point, search, or appeal to the paper occurs in `verify`.
+
+## Construction and Track B hardness
+
+Generation samples equal-size variable classes and one capped monomial per
+class first. Intersecting their irreducible pure-power ideals gives, by direct
+distributive reasoning, exactly the published generators: one cap-crossing pure
+power per variable and `x_i*x_j` for every cross-class pair. Thus the answer is
+known before the input ideal is assembled.
+
+Track A would be false. Section 3 gives a terminating Slice Algorithm, Section
+5 supplies pivot strategies, and Section 7 benchmarks Frobby against
+Alexander-dual and Scarf-complex implementations. For this narrower “find one”
+task, an even simpler reference algorithm scans the quadratic generators
+incident to one anchor and takes its non-neighbours. At the hard preset it
+solves 8/8 instances in about 0.0003 seconds after 7,680 pair inspections and
+15,240 endpoint comparisons, with complexity `O(|min(I)| + n)`.
+
+The intended no-tool route ignores that 67,240-character generator list.
+Section 4.7 observes that exponent order rather than magnitude controls the
+decomposition. Here the caps also carry a planted affine invariant: modulo the
+largest cap, `c_i - i*c_1` is constant on each coordinate class. Bucketing 128
+caps takes at most 256 exact field operations and yields any valid support. The
+gap is therefore 7,680 list inspections versus 256 arithmetic operations, if
+the invariant is noticed.
+
+The paper’s easy regimes were checked explicitly. Section 3.2’s square-free
+base case does not apply because the ideal has non-square-free pure powers;
+Section 4.2’s independence split does not apply because the mixed generators
+connect every nontrivial variable partition; Section 4.3’s two-variable formula
+does not apply; and the generator count is far above Section 4.1’s simplified
+`|min(I)| <= n` base case. Large exponent digits are not claimed as hardness,
+in accord with Section 4.7.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(seed=0, n=6, block_size=2, calibration_prime=13)`, the full
+rendered mathematical data are:
+
+```text
+K[x_1,...,x_6], with pure generators x_i^(c_i+1).
+caps: 1:8 2:3 3:10 4:11 5:13 6:1
+quadratic generators:
+(4,5) (2,3) (3,4) (2,5) (1,3) (1,4)
+(5,6) (1,5) (1,6) (3,6) (2,4) (2,6)
+Find a maximal standard monomial with exactly two nonzero factors.
+Return [[i_1,e_1],[i_2,e_2]] with increasing 1-based indices and e_t=c_(i_t).
+```
+
+The missing quadratic pair `(3,5)` gives
+`<answer>[[3,10],[5,13]]</answer>`. The module reports
+`verify(inst, inst["answer"]) == (True, "ok")`. Dropping the second factor
+reports `(False, "expected exactly 2 nonzero factors")`. This demo is genuinely
+hand-solvable: list the three missing pairs and choose either one.
+
+## Presets
+
+| preset | n | support size | prime | minimal generators | candidate space | rendered chars |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| demo | 6 | 2 | 13 | 18 | 15 | 1,703 |
+| easy | 96 | 8 | 193 | 4,320 | 132,601,016,340 | 35,744 |
+| medium | 112 | 8 | 227 | 5,936 | 475,491,062,970 | 50,060 |
+| hard (candidate shipping) | 128 | 8 | 257 | 7,808 | 1,429,702,652,400 | 67,240 |
+
+No preset was rejected by a local gate. External model hardening did not reach
+a verdict because of the credential failure, so `hard` is provisional rather
+than a released shipping level.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses verify; answers are JSON-native |
+| G2 | pass | empty, drop, swap, duplicate, and out-of-range corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered from prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; exact density `16 / 1,429,702,652,400 = 1.1191e-11` |
+| G5 | pass | 16 exact shipping answers; reference cost 7,680 pair inspections and 15,240 endpoint comparisons |
+| G6 | pass | 0/8 successes for each of 6 attacks; reference and compact algorithms both solve 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 | pass | n=256 builds and verifies; answer remains 16 atomic integers; space grows from 41 to 49 bits |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 relabellings invariant and valid; 20/20 unrelated seeds have distinct keys |
+| G9(c) | pass | 77 worst-case measured characters, 20 estimated tokens, 16 atoms, 256 intended operations |
+
+The six failing attacks are largest caps, smallest caps, every circular
+contiguous window, index-residue classes, the unit-slope affine ansatz, and 256
+uniform support restarts. All were run over eight independent shipping seeds.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+| arm | preset | calls | completed attempts | solved | outcome |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 4 | 0 | 0 | all HTTP 403; no hardening verdict |
+| structural hint | hard-only scratch copy | 4 | 0 | 0 | all HTTP 403 |
+| placebo hint | hard-only scratch copy | 4 | 0 | 0 | all HTTP 403 |
+
+The retained bare seeds are `794472659`, `1966976507`, `1343724116`, and
+`1300515103`;
+each row in `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl` has `solved="error"`. Since neither G9
+arm completed an attempt, hinted-minus-placebo is undefined, not zero, and no
+conclusion about the claimed invariant can be drawn. The size/effort part of G9
+still passes independently.
+
+## Use
+
+From this directory:
+
+```python
+import gen_0806_3680 as g
+
+params = g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=42, **params)
+question = g.render(inst)
+candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+Run local gates with `python3 gen_0806_3680.py`. From the repository root, emit
+instances with `bash scripts/emit.sh 0806.3680 20 hard`. Do not run
+`scripts/submit.sh` until `python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_0806_3680.py`
+completes with a real `hardened` verdict and the G9 arms are rerun.
+
+## Caveats
+
+The exact random-guess density applies to the declared prior: a uniform
+eight-variable support with exponents already fixed to their caps. It says
+nothing about a solver using cap residues or the mixed-generator incidence
+pattern. Indeed both routes solve every instance efficiently with tools; that
+is why this is Track B.
+
+Frobby, Macaulay 2, Monos, a modern CAS, and a full Slice implementation were
+not run. They compute more than this one-witness task, while the reported
+incidence scan is already a stronger specialized baseline for this distribution.
+The modular cap invariant is generator-specific rather than a theorem of the
+paper, although the inverse intersection and witness definition are native.
+The large rendered pair list may test visual bookkeeping as well as insight;
+the fixed 77-character witness and 256-operation compact route keep it within
+the stated no-tool caps. Most importantly, no external model-hardness evidence
+exists until the OpenRouter quota is restored.
