     1	# arXiv:0806.3680 problem generator
     2	
     3	## Profile and trust status
     4	
     5	| field | value |
     6	|---|---|
     7	| track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     8	| native domain | algebra |
     9	| object regime | integer lattice |
    10	| computational core | other (exact monomial divisibility and support structure) |
    11	| certificate | exact symbolic sparse monomial |
    12	| intuition | invariant: affine residues of the pure-power caps reveal the concealed coordinate classes |
    13	| domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
    14	| candidate shipping preset | `hard` |
    15	
    16	The generator and every local gate pass. The required external hardness claim is
    17	**not yet established**: on 2026-09-05 the repository-owned hardening harness
    18	made four bare, four hinted, and four placebo calls, but the supplied OpenRouter
    19	credential returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” every time. Errors correctly
    20	count as neither solved nor failed. The harness-owned transcripts are retained,
    21	but this result must not be submitted until a funded key produces a real
    22	`hardened` verdict.
    23	
    24	## The problem
    25	
    26	The source is Bjarke Hammersholt Roune, [“The Slice Algorithm For Irreducible
    27	Decomposition of Monomial Ideals”](https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3680). The solver
    28	receives the complete minimal generating set of an artinian monomial ideal:
    29	pure powers `x_i^(c_i+1)` and explicitly listed quadratic monomials `x_i*x_j`.
    30	It must return one maximal standard monomial `d`, meaning `d` is outside the
    31	ideal while every `d*x_i` is inside it. Section 2.2 identifies these monomials
    32	as a socle basis and maps them bijectively to irreducible components. The answer
    33	is a sparse list of its eight nonzero variable/exponent pairs.
    34	
    35	Verification is independent of the plant. It checks the answer grammar and
    36	caps, proves no displayed generator divides `d`, and proves a displayed
    37	generator divides each `d*x_i`. These are exact integer comparisons; no CAS,
    38	floating point, search, or appeal to the paper occurs in `verify`.
    39	
    40	## Construction and Track B hardness
    41	
    42	Generation samples equal-size variable classes and one capped monomial per
    43	class first. Intersecting their irreducible pure-power ideals gives, by direct
    44	distributive reasoning, exactly the published generators: one cap-crossing pure
    45	power per variable and `x_i*x_j` for every cross-class pair. Thus the answer is
    46	known before the input ideal is assembled.
    47	
    48	Track A would be false. Section 3 gives a terminating Slice Algorithm, Section
    49	5 supplies pivot strategies, and Section 7 benchmarks Frobby against
    50	Alexander-dual and Scarf-complex implementations. For this narrower “find one”
    51	task, an even simpler reference algorithm scans the quadratic generators
    52	incident to one anchor and takes its non-neighbours. At the hard preset it
    53	solves 8/8 instances in about 0.0003 seconds after 7,680 pair inspections and
    54	15,240 endpoint comparisons, with complexity `O(|min(I)| + n)`.
    55	
    56	The intended no-tool route ignores that 67,240-character generator list.
    57	Section 4.7 observes that exponent order rather than magnitude controls the
    58	decomposition. Here the caps also carry a planted affine invariant: modulo the
    59	largest cap, `c_i - i*c_1` is constant on each coordinate class. Bucketing 128
    60	caps takes at most 256 exact field operations and yields any valid support. The
    61	gap is therefore 7,680 list inspections versus 256 arithmetic operations, if
    62	the invariant is noticed.
    63	
    64	The paper’s easy regimes were checked explicitly. Section 3.2’s square-free
    65	base case does not apply because the ideal has non-square-free pure powers;
    66	Section 4.2’s independence split does not apply because the mixed generators
    67	connect every nontrivial variable partition; Section 4.3’s two-variable formula
    68	does not apply; and the generator count is far above Section 4.1’s simplified
    69	`|min(I)| <= n` base case. Large exponent digits are not claimed as hardness,
    70	in accord with Section 4.7.
    71	
    72	## Worked demo
    73	
    74	For `make_instance(seed=0, n=6, block_size=2, calibration_prime=13)`, the full
    75	rendered instance is:
    76	
    77	```text
    78	Find a maximal standard monomial of an exact monomial ideal.
    79	
    80	Definitions. Work in a polynomial ring K[x_1,...,x_6] over an arbitrary
    81	field K. A monomial is x_1^a_1...x_6^a_6 with nonnegative integer
    82	exponents. A monomial u divides v when every exponent of u is at most the
    83	corresponding exponent of v. The monomial ideal generated by a list G contains
    84	exactly the monomials divisible by at least one member of G. A monomial d is
    85	standard when d is not in the ideal. It is maximal standard when d is standard
    86	but d*x_i belongs to the ideal for every i=1,...,6.
    87	
